  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Friday)\nIssued at 1144 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nA chilly Fall day is on display across the Mid-South with current\ntemperatures in the 50s under mostly clear skies as a 1024mb high\npressure continues to build over the Middle Mississippi Valley.\nWith ample sunshine, highs this afternoon will top out in the mid\n50s to lower 60s. As we head into the overnight hours, patchy fog\nand early morning frost is expected as winds go calm with ample\nradiational cooling, with best chances existing north of the\nTennessee and Mississippi line. We'll also wake up to a crisp\nmorning with latest NBM probabilities hinting 40-60% chance for\nfreezing temperatures across the Missouri Bootheel, northern\nportions of northeast Arkansas, and much of West Tennessee.\nThough dense fog is not anticipated overnight, freezing fog\ncannot be ruled out, especially in the aforementioned areas.\nEither way, early morning commuters should be cautious on the\nroads. Fog will mix out by mid-morning as winds increase.\n\nBenign weather is expected through early week as aforementioned\nhigh pressure remains fixed over the region. Temperatures will\nbegin to moderate into midweek with highs in the upper 60s to\nlower 70s and lows generally in the 40s beginning Monday. Very\nweak upper-level troughing will push into the Lower Mississippi\nValley Tuesday potentially providing enough moisture for a few\nlight showers Tuesday into Wednesday. If precipitation does make\nit to the ground, appreciable rainfall is not expected. A\nsecondary, more notable and deepening, upper-level trough and\nattendant cold front will push in from the Midwest Thursday.\nForecast PWATs begin to surge to around 1.3\", nearing the 90th\npercentile for this time of the year. This will be our best shot\nat precipitation over the next 7 days, though, due to uncertainty\nin the overall evolution of this trough, chances of precipitation\nare 25-40% Wednesday into Thursday. Temperatures behind this\nfront will plummet once again with lows in the 30s and 40s and\nhighs in the 50s to lower 60s as a much cooler, dry airmass\nbuilds in behind the front. Fall has arrived and is here to stay.\n\nAEH",
